    "rich_text_intro": {
      "type": "rich-text",
      "blocks": {
        "text_intro": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p>Nothing says fall like apple cider and brown butter, and this dish has both. Poaching is the gentlest way to cook a steelhead loin &mdash; held just below a simmer, it stays silky instead of firming up the way a hot pan makes it. It feels like something you&rsquo;d find in a casual French kitchen: simple and elegant, and it would pair beautifully with a few cheeses for dessert.</p><p>Two numbers carry the whole thing. The poaching liquid wants to sit at 160 to 175&deg;F, which is lower than most people think &ldquo;gentle&rdquo; means. And the brown butter has to come off the heat before the mustard goes in, or the sauce splits and will not come back.</p>",
            "text_size": "subtext-md",
            "text_max_size": 32,
            "text_font": "body",
            "secondary_color": false,
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        }
      },
