    "faq_steelhead": {
      "type": "faq",
      "blocks": {
        "q_temp": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "What does &ldquo;just below a simmer&rdquo; actually mean?",
            "text": "<p>160 to 175&deg;F. The surface should look almost still &mdash; a wisp of steam, maybe a shimmer, but no bubbles breaking through. Most recipes that say gentle simmer mean 185 to 200&deg;F, which is hot enough to squeeze moisture out of the loin and leave you with the chalky texture poaching exists to avoid.</p>",
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        },
        "q_done": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "How do I know when the fish is done?",
            "text": "<p>About 120&deg;F at the thickest point, or when the flesh has just turned opaque and yields to a knife tip without resistance. Six to eight minutes depending on thickness. It carries on cooking as it rests, so pull it a touch before you think it is ready.</p>",
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        },
        "q_split": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "My sauce split. What went wrong?",
            "text": "<p>The butter was still hot when the mustard went in. Take the pan off the heat entirely and give it a minute before you whisk in the Dijon &mdash; warm butter holds the emulsion, hot butter breaks it. Once it has separated it will not come back, but a fresh tablespoon of butter whisked in off the heat will usually rescue most of it.</p>",
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        },
        "q_cider": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Fresh cider or hard cider?",
            "text": "<p>This is built for fresh, unfiltered, unsweetened cider, which is sweet and gives a rounder sauce. Dry hard cider also works and gives something sharper and more savoury &mdash; if you use it, pull back on the lemon at the end or the sauce tips too far acidic.</p>",
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        },
        "q_ahead": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Can I make any of it ahead?",
            "text": "<p>The leeks, yes &mdash; up to two days, and they improve. Warm them through while the cider comes up to temperature. The fish and the sauce are both last-minute: poached fish goes grainy on reheating and brown butter sauce sets and splits once it cools.</p>",
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        },
        "q_thaw": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "How should I thaw Seatopia seafood?",
            "text": "<p>Overnight in the refrigerator in the sealed pack, about 12 hours, then pat it dry before it goes anywhere near the pan. In a hurry, submerge the sealed pack in a bowl of chilled water for 20 to 30 minutes, changing the water once. Never at room temperature.</p>",
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        }
      },
      "block_order": [
        "q_temp",
        "q_done",
        "q_split",
        "q_cider",
        "q_ahead",
        "q_thaw"
      ],
